  "reasoning": "The patient presents with a red, itchy, painful, and blistering rash on the face, accompanied by flu-like symptoms including fever and body pain. Evaluating the possible diagnoses step by step:\n1. Impetigo typically presents with weeping sores or honey-colored crusts, usually localized around the nose and mouth, but does not prominently feature systemic flu-like symptoms like significant body pain.\n2. Allergy and drug reaction generally present with itching or flaking rashes but lack the combination of acute blistering, notable pain, and fever/body aches.\n3. Chicken pox classically presents with an itchy, painful vesicular (blistering) rash and is frequently accompanied by systemic flu-like symptoms such as fever and body aches/fatigue. This closely matches the patient's description of a blistering facial rash with fever and body pain, aligning perfectly with the reference case for chicken pox which notes a rash with itchy/painful bumps alongside fever and tiredness.",
  "final_answer": "[DIAGNOSIS]chicken pox[/DIAGNOSIS]"
}
